\title{The Topology of Harmony: Cyclic Structure of Musical Intervals
and the Maximality of the Circle of Fifths}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous algebraic--topological model of tonal harmony in
twelve-tone equal temperament and use it to prove, from first principles, the
special status of the circle of fifths. Encoding the twelve pitch classes as
the cyclic group $\PC = \ZZ/12\ZZ$, we define the \emph{harmonic cycle length}
of an interval of $k$ semitones as the additive order of $k$, the number of
distinct pitch classes reached by stacking the interval. We establish the
closed form $\cyclelen(k) = 12/\gcd(12,k)$ and derive from it the complete
inventory of harmonic cycles: the perfect fifth and semitone span all twelve
pitch classes, the whole tone spans six, the minor third four, the major third
three, and the tritone two. We prove that every cycle length divides twelve (a
Lagrange-type constraint), that no interval generates a longer cycle than the
perfect fifth, and that an interval spans the full chromatic aggregate if and
only if it is coprime to twelve---the maximal generators being exactly
$\{1,5,7,11\}$. We exhibit the circle of fifths as an explicit Hamiltonian
cycle on pitch-class space and interpret it as the longest one-dimensional
cycle ($H_1$ generator) in the harmonic space. Finally, we introduce a
normalized persistence-bar length $\barlen(k) = \cyclelen(k)/12 \in (0,1]$ and
prove the separating thresholds $\barlen(7) = 1 > 1/2$ and $\barlen(6) = 1/6 <
1/2$. Everything generalizes verbatim to $n$-tone equal temperament, where the
longest cycle has length $n$ and is attained by the $\varphi(n)$ intervals
coprime to $n$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The circle of fifths is arguably the single most important organizing
principle of Western tonal harmony. Empirically, iterating the perfect fifth
traverses all twelve pitch classes before returning to its origin, and this
loop underwrites key relationships, modulation, and the sense of tonal
``distance.'' Our aim is to strip this observation down to its mathematical
core and prove, rather than assert, why the fifth occupies a privileged
position---and to frame the result in the language of shape, so that the
informal talk of harmonic ``motion'' and ``cycles'' acquires a literal,
topological meaning.

The guiding conjecture from the applied side is topological: if one forms the
point cloud of chords appearing in a piece of music and computes its persistent
homology, then genuinely tonal music (the chorale being the paradigm) exhibits
a long-lived first homology class $H_1$---a persistent one-dimensional
cycle---corresponding to circular harmonic motion along the circle of fifths,
whereas music built on short harmonic cycles or without a consistent generating
interval exhibits only short-lived or absent $H_1$ classes. This paper
formalizes the \emph{algebraic skeleton} underlying that picture: the cycles
themselves, their exact lengths, the maximality of the fifth, and normalized
bar-length thresholds that separate the regimes. We treat the length of the
cyclic subgroup generated by an interval as a faithful combinatorial proxy for
the length of the corresponding $H_1$ persistence bar.

\section{Pitch-class space and harmonic cycles}

\begin{definition}[Pitch-class space]
Under octave equivalence---the identification of pitches differing by whole
octaves---the chromatic scale collapses to twelve \emph{pitch classes}, modeled
as the cyclic group
\[
\PC := \ZZ/12\ZZ = \{0,1,2,\dots,11\},
\]
with addition modulo $12$, where $0=\mathrm{C},\ 1=\mathrm{C}\sharp,\
2=\mathrm{D},\ \dots,\ 11=\mathrm{B}$. The group $\PC$ has exactly twelve
elements.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Interval]
An \emph{interval} of $k$ semitones is the group element $k \bmod 12 \in \PC$.
Salient intervals include the semitone ($k=1$), whole tone ($k=2$), minor third
($k=3$), major third ($k=4$), perfect fourth ($k=5$), tritone ($k=6$), and
perfect fifth ($k=7$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Harmonic cycle length]
\emph{Stacking} an interval $k$ means repeatedly adding it: $0,\ k,\ 2k,\ 3k,
\dots \pmod{12}$. The \emph{harmonic cycle length} of $k$,
\[
\cyclelen(k) := \ord(k),
\]
is the additive order of $k$ in $\PC$: the smallest positive integer $m$ with
$m\cdot k \equiv 0 \pmod{12}$, equivalently the number of distinct pitch classes
visited before the loop closes, and the cardinality of the cyclic subgroup
$\langle k\rangle \le \PC$. It is our combinatorial proxy for the length of the
one-dimensional persistence bar of the gesture ``keep transposing by~$k$.''
\end{definition}

\section{The cycle-length formula and the harmonic inventory}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For every $k \in \NN$,
\[
\cyclelen(k) = \frac{12}{\gcd(12,k)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The additive order of $k$ in $\ZZ/n\ZZ$ is $n/\gcd(n,k)$: the multiples of $k$
modulo $n$ are exactly the multiples of $d:=\gcd(n,k)$, of which there are
$n/d$, and this many steps are needed to return to $0$. Specialize $n=12$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Harmonic inventory]\label{cor:inv}
The musical intervals produce the following cycles.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
Interval & $k$ & $\gcd(12,k)$ & $\cyclelen(k)$ & Musical object \\
\midrule
Semitone & $1$ & $1$ & $12$ & chromatic scale \\
Whole tone & $2$ & $2$ & $6$ & whole-tone scale \\
Minor third & $3$ & $3$ & $4$ & diminished-seventh chord \\
Major third & $4$ & $4$ & $3$ & augmented triad \\
Perfect fourth & $5$ & $1$ & $12$ & full aggregate \\
Tritone & $6$ & $6$ & $2$ & tritone dyad \\
Perfect fifth & $7$ & $1$ & $12$ & circle of fifths \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation of Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} at each $k$.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural results}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform bound]\label{thm:bound}
For every $k$, $\cyclelen(k) \le 12$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\cyclelen(k) = 12/\gcd(12,k) \le 12$ since $\gcd(12,k)\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximality of the fifth]\label{thm:max}
For every interval $k$, $\cyclelen(k) \le \cyclelen(7) = 12$. No interval
generates a longer harmonic cycle than the circle of fifths.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:inv} ($\cyclelen(7)=12$) with
Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divisibility---a Lagrange constraint]\label{thm:dvd}
Every harmonic cycle length divides twelve: $\cyclelen(k) \mid 12$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $\gcd(12,k)\mid 12$, its cofactor $12/\gcd(12,k)$ also divides $12$.
Equivalently, $\langle k\rangle \le \PC$ has order dividing $|\PC|=12$ by
Lagrange's theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spanning $\iff$ coprimality]\label{thm:span}
$\cyclelen(k) = 12 \iff \gcd(12,k) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\gcd(12,k)=1$ then $\cyclelen(k)=12$. Conversely, with $d=\gcd(12,k)$ we
have $d\cdot(12/d)=12$; if $12/d=12$ then $d=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Maximal generators]\label{cor:gen}
Among the residues $0\le k<12$, exactly $k\in\{1,5,7,11\}$ satisfy
$\cyclelen(k)=12$: the semitone, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, and major
seventh. The perfect fifth $k=7$ is the classical circle-of-fifths
representative.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:span} these are the $k<12$ coprime to $12$; a finite check
over $k=0,\dots,11$ confirms the set is $\{1,5,7,11\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Generation]\label{thm:generate}
The subgroup generated by the perfect fifth is the whole group,
$\langle 7\rangle = \PC$. More generally $\langle k\rangle = \PC$ if and only if
$\gcd(12,k)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\langle k\rangle$ has cardinality $\cyclelen(k)$, which equals $12=|\PC|$
exactly when $\gcd(12,k)=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:span}. For $k=7$ this holds, so
the fifth generates $\PC$: from any note, stacking fifths reaches every pitch
class.
\end{proof}

\section{The circle of fifths as an explicit Hamiltonian cycle}

\begin{definition}[Circle of fifths]
Let
\[
\cof := (0,\,7,\,2,\,9,\,4,\,11,\,6,\,1,\,8,\,3,\,10,\,5),
\]
the sequence obtained by stacking perfect fifths from $0$; its $i$-th entry is
$7i \bmod 12$ for $i=0,\dots,11$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hamiltonicity]\label{thm:ham}
The sequence $\cof$ has length $12$, is duplicate-free, and contains every pitch
class. Hence it visits every vertex of pitch-class space exactly once and closes
up---a Hamiltonian cycle on $\PC$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Length twelve is immediate. Since $7$ is a unit modulo $12$, the map $i\mapsto
7i \bmod 12$ is a bijection of $\ZZ/12\ZZ$; hence the entries are distinct and
their image is all of $\PC$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Topological interpretation.} A noncontractible loop in the
harmonic space is a generator of the first homology $H_1$---a one-dimensional
hole. Theorem~\ref{thm:ham} realizes the circle of fifths as an explicit closed
loop threading every vertex exactly once; by Theorem~\ref{thm:max} it is the
longest interval-generated cycle available, i.e.\ the persistent $H_1$ generator
of harmony.

\section{Normalized persistence bars and separating thresholds}

\begin{definition}[Normalized persistence-bar length]
For an interval $k$, $\barlen(k) := \cyclelen(k)/12 \in (0,1]$, the normalized
$H_1$-bar length: $1$ means ``sweeps the entire tonal universe in one loop,''
and values near $0$ mean ``closes immediately.''
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Range and maximum]\label{thm:range}
For every $k$, $\barlen(k)\le 1$, and $\barlen(7)=1$ attains the maximum, with
$\barlen(k)\le\barlen(7)$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\cyclelen(k)\le 12$ gives $\barlen(k)\le 1$; and $\cyclelen(7)=12$ gives
$\barlen(7)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separating thresholds]\label{thm:thresh}
\[
\barlen(7) = 1 > \tfrac12, \qquad \barlen(6) = \tfrac16 < \tfrac12,
\]
and $\barlen(6) < \barlen(7)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\barlen(7)=12/12=1>1/2$ and $\barlen(6)=2/12=1/6<1/2$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} Harmonic motion organized around the circle
of fifths registers a normalized bar of length $1$, well above $1/2$: a long,
persistent $H_1$ class. Motion built on short cycles such as the tritone
registers a bar of length $1/6$, well below $1/2$; and writing without a
consistent generating interval yields no long-lived $H_1$ class. This is the
algebraic skeleton of the predicted separation between far-reaching tonal
harmony ($>0.5$), intermediate short-cycle harmony ($0.2$--$0.5$), and atonal
harmony (near $0$).

\section{Discussion and generalizations}

\textbf{$n$-tone temperament.} Replacing $\PC$ by $\ZZ/n\ZZ$ gives
$\cyclelen_n(k)=n/\gcd(n,k)$, with maximum $n$ attained exactly by the
$\varphi(n)$ intervals coprime to $n$. Thus $19$-TET has $\varphi(19)=18$
generators and $31$-TET has $\varphi(31)=30$; the circle of fifths is one
instance of a universal law.

\textbf{From proxy to genuine persistence.} The natural next step replaces the
cyclic-subgroup proxy with the persistent homology of a Vietoris--Rips
filtration on a finite metric pitch-class space (e.g.\ the torus metric
$d(a,b)=\min(|a-b|,12-|a-b|)$), proving that the $H_1$ class born from
circle-of-fifths motion has the longest bar. The interval-$k$ motion is the
circulant graph $\mathrm{Cay}(\ZZ/12\ZZ,\{\pm k\})$, whose first Betti number
$b_1 = |E|-|V|+(\text{components})$ connects directly to $\cyclelen$.

\section{Conclusion}

From octave equivalence alone, tonal harmony carries a precise and provable
shape: cycle length obeys $12/\gcd(12,k)$; lengths are exactly the divisors of
twelve; the perfect fifth generates the whole pitch-class group and realizes an
explicit Hamiltonian loop through all twelve tones; and a normalized bar length
cleanly separates far-reaching circular harmony (fifth, bar $=1$) from short,
local harmony (tritone, bar $=1/6$). The circle of fifths is, in a literal
topological sense, the fundamental loop of harmony.

